    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d402">

                <pb facs="0407" n="395" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg395"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.51A/1087: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Iran</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ford</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-11T16:00:00+03:25">April 11, 1944—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>249. Debate on Cabinet was resumed today.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d402-f1"/> Deputy
                    Kazemi spoke at length and bitterly against Millspaugh, emphasizing what he
                    called Millspaugh’s dictatorial attitude and demanding that he be removed.
                    Kazemi likewise criticized subordinate members of Mission by name, among them
                    Solomon (Financial Agent in Khlizistan), Vivian (now Director General of
                    Industrial Development), Shuckman <pb facs="0408" n="396" xml:id="pg396"/>
                    (Director of Movements), and [omission] (assistant in Price Stabilization
                    Section).</p>
                <p>Deputies Etebar and Keshavar also criticized Millspaugh but in milder vein.</p>
                <p>No vote was taken today. Debate is to be continued April 13.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d402-f1" n="86" xml:id="d402-f1-content">
                <p>Mohammed Saed had replaced Ali Soheily as Prime Minister, and had organized a new
                    government on April 6, 1944.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
